-- define the array outside the component or wrap in `useMemo` to avoid unnecessary re-renders"],[1615,"**Missing scopes.** The OAuth client requests `api:read-data`, `api:write-data`, `api:use-ontologies-read`, and `api:use-ontologies-write` by default. Other APIs need more. For example, the user and marking hooks in `@osdk/react/platform-apis` need `api:admin-read`. Add the scope to the application in Developer Console, then request it:"],[950,"**Never mutate an object to update the UI.** Assigning to `employee.jobTitle` throws in strict mode. Apply an action, then fetch again."],[1478,"**Normalized object cache.** Each object, such as `Employee:42`, is stored once. When it changes, every list, link, and detail view that contains it re-renders with the new values."],[6614,"**Number input** (auto-detected): For numeric properties (`double`, `integer`, `long`, `float`, `decimal`, `byte`, `short`)"],[299,"**One runtime.** Several generated SDKs share the same `@osdk/client` code instead of each shipping its own."],[1481,"**Optimistic updates with rollback.** Pass `$optimisticUpdate` to `applyAction` to show the expected result immediately. If the action fails, the cache restores the previous state."],[2805,"**Optimistic updates.** They are removed when the action finishes. See Optimistic updates."],[4941,"**OSDK Component Tokens**: Component-specific semantic tokens"],[6073,"**OSDK layer** (`CbacPicker`, `CbacPickerDialog`) — fetches data using `@osdk/react` hooks, converts it to primitive props, passes to the base layer"],[6964,"**OSDK tokens (`--osdk-*`)** — every visual property used inside OSDK components resolves through a token prefixed with `--osdk-` (e.g. `--osdk-table-header-bg`, `--osdk-form-section-content-padding-inline`). Override these to theme **OSDK components only**, leaving other Blueprint components in your app untouched."],[6877,"**Outline** (`\"outline\"`) — Shows the document's table of contents. If the PDF has embedded bookmarks, those are displayed with their original nesting and bold/italic styling. If no bookmarks exist, headings are automatically extracted from the text content based on font size."],[5942,"**Override at the right level**:"],[5939,"**Override Blueprint tokens** to change the entire design system"],[5938,"**Override OSDK tokens directly** for quick customization"],[6891,"**Page navigation** — previous/next buttons and a page number input"],[6360,"**Persist filter state with `defaultFilterStates`** -- read changes out via `onFilterStateChanged`, key them with the exported `getFilterKey(definition)`, and pass them back through `defaultFilterStates` to rehydrate after navigation. `serializeFilterStates` / `deserializeFilterStates` handle the `Date` round-trip for you"],[1322,"**pnpm.** The plugin runs `maker`, `osdk`, and `tsc` with `pnpm exec`, so pnpm must be on your `PATH` and each binary must be installed in your project."],[1386,"**Primary key.** Must be a primitive type, such as `string` or `integer`, and cannot be `editOnly`. Every object needs a unique value."],[1606,"**Proxy through Vite.** Forward `/api` and `/multipass/api` to Foundry from the dev server, and set `VITE_FOUNDRY_API_URL=http://localhost:8080`. Requests then go to your own origin, so CORS does not apply. See Proxy Foundry requests in development."],[1610,"**Redirect URL.** The redirect URL matches one listed on the same page exactly, including protocol, port, and path, such as `http://localhost:8080/auth/callback`."],[2923,"**Requests and cache.** The devtools wrap the observable client that `OsdkProvider` creates and record the cache reads, network requests, and actions that pass through it. Data you load with raw `fetch`, `axios`, or another state library does not appear."],[6068,"**Required marking groups** — which additional markings must be selected for the classification to be valid"],[798,"**Resolves `@osdk` packages with the `browser`, `require`, and `import` conditions**, so Metro picks their browser builds on every platform."],[1617,"**Resources outside the application's scope.** The object type, action, or function is not included in the application's Ontology SDK resources in Developer Console."],[6893,"**Rotate controls** — rotate all pages left (counter-clockwise) or right (clockwise) in 90° increments"],[6895,"**Search button** — opens the search bar"],[2859,"**Search ontology** matches component names, object types, primary keys, actions, and property names."],[6887,"**Shift+Enter** — previous match"],[1079,"**Ship.** The IR file is the artifact. None of these packages uploads or installs it into a Foundry stack."],[6894,"**Sidebar toggle** — open/close the sidebar panel"],[298,"**Smaller bundles.** Each definition is a small named export. Bundlers drop the ones you do not import, so the SDK's cost scales with what you use, not with the size of the Ontology."],[949,"**Store results in `shallowRef`.** OSDK objects are frozen. Vue does not wrap frozen objects in reactive proxies, so changes are never detected inside them anyway. Replace the whole value when new data arrives."],[6562,"**Supported editor components:**"],[6613,"**Text input** (default): For string properties"],[6876,"**Thumbnails** (`\"thumbnails\"`) — Renders page thumbnails. Click a thumbnail to jump to that page."],[1387,"**Title.** Must be a primitive type, or a struct whose `mainValue` is primitive."],[1612,"**Token provider.** The client receives the OAuth client as its token provider: `createClient(foundryUrl, ontologyRid, auth)`."],[1480,"**Updates after actions.** An action's response lists the objects it added, modified, and deleted. The cache reloads those objects and re-evaluates affected lists against their `where` clauses, with no manual invalidation."],[1008,"**Upgrade them in one command.** Bump the three packages together, and include `@osdk/react-components` if you use it:"],[6363,"**Use `addFilterMode=\"uncontrolled\"` for progressive disclosure** -- start with a few visible filters and let users add more as needed"],[5941,"**Use CSS Layers**: Always import the default tokens into a layer, then add your custom overrides in a higher-priority layer. This ensures your custom theme takes precedence without specificity issues."],[6361,"**Use objectSet constraints to scope filter values** -- pass a prefiltered objectSet (e.g. `client(Employee).where(...)`) so filter dropdowns only show relevant values"],[206,"**Use the devtools.** The Cache panel shows entries, hits, and misses in a running app."],[1618,"**User permissions.** The signed-in user cannot see the data or apply the action. Requests made with a public OAuth client act as the signed-in user and never exceed that user's access."],[6069,"**Validation (`isValid`)** — whether the current selection satisfies all constraints"],[6617,"**Validation**:"],[1323,"**Vite** and a Node.js version that can import `.mts` files natively: 22.18 or later, or 23.6 or later."],[1009,"**Your generated SDK has a peer dependency on `@osdk/client`.** It is set to the client version that generated it, so install an `@osdk/client` that satisfies the SDK's peer range. When you upgrade the client across many versions, generate a new SDK version too."],[6892,"**Zoom controls** — zoom in/out in 25% steps (range: 25%–500%)"],[1096,"`--apiNamespace com.example` prefixes the API names of the object types, interfaces, shared property types, and action types you define, so `Employee` becomes `com.example.Employee`. Pass names without the prefix in your definitions. Link type API names are not prefixed, and value types are prefixed only when you set `namespacePrefix: true`."],[554,"`--ontologyPath` takes an Ontology metadata file written by `--ontologyWritePath`."],[553,"`--ontologyWritePath` saves the loaded metadata so you can regenerate later without signing in."],[443,"`--token <token>`"],[447,"`--token` and `--tokenFile` cannot be used together. The token must be a JWT. Without one, the command exits with `No token found.`"],[444,"`--tokenFile <path>`, a file whose contents are the token"],[214,"`.validation`, not `.message`"],[1575,"`\"<name>\" is not exported by @osdk/client/…/observable.js`"],[6335,"`\"FILTER_LIST_INITIALIZED\"` reports the state seeded from `defaultFilterStates`\nand from each definition's `defaultFilterState`, so the initial clause and\nfiltered `ObjectSet` arrive without waiting for a first interaction. If you only\ncare about the current state and not what caused it, ignore `reason` — every\n`snapshot` carries the full `filterClause` and `filteredObjectSet`:"],[6805,"`\"system\"` is the only mode where the resolved theme can change without anyone calling `setTheme` — it reacts to the OS preference flipping while your app is open."],[3757,"`() => Promise<string>`: resolves to `\"PUBLIC\"`."],[1986,"`@osdk/agents` is for code that runs inside a Foundry agent, not for apps that call one. Foundry injects an Ontology `Client` and a `PlatformClient` into each invocation; `@osdk/agents/experimental` reads them."],[3712,"`@osdk/aip-core`"],[2244,"`@osdk/aip-core` is at version 0.x and its API can change in any minor release. Two limits shape this page:"],[2011,"`@osdk/aip-core` is at version 0.x, and its API can change in any minor release. The current release runs one model call per request: there is no built-in multi-step tool loop, no structured (JSON) output, and only text content parts are sent. Image and file parts are dropped with a warning."],[2010,"`@osdk/aip-core` sends chat completions to the Foundry Language Model Service (LMS) with the same auth, governance, and usage tracking as the rest of your OSDK app. Use `generateText` for a complete answer and `streamText` to render tokens as they arrive."],[711,"`@osdk/api`, `@osdk/client`, and `@osdk/react` are released together with one version number. Install the same version of all three, and keep prereleases together. Your generated SDK has a peer dependency on `@osdk/client`, so generate a new SDK version when you upgrade the client across many versions."],[1595,"`@osdk/api`, `@osdk/client`, and `@osdk/react` share one version number. Install the same version of all three, and keep prereleases together:"],[375,"`@osdk/cli` as a dev dependency."],[424,"`@osdk/cli` deploys your app to Foundry website hosting, manages its versions, publishes widget sets, keeps branched SDKs in sync, and generates SDKs from an Ontology. This page lists every command and flag."],[3710,"`@osdk/client`"],[655,"`@osdk/client` and `@osdk/api` are peer dependencies as well; install them as described in Installation."],[726,"`@osdk/client` changelog"],[2012,"`@osdk/client` is a peer dependency. `@osdk/aip-core` brings in `@osdk/language-models` itself."],[4456,"`@osdk/client` reads and edits your Ontology from any JavaScript runtime with `fetch`: Node.js scripts and servers, serverless functions, and browser apps in any framework. It has no UI dependency. Your generated SDK supplies typed definitions for your object types, interfaces, actions, and functions, and the client runs them."],[814,"`@osdk/client` runs in Node.js without a browser or bundler, so you can query the Ontology and apply actions from scripts, backend services, scheduled jobs, and serverless functions. This page covers what changes outside the browser: authentication, module format, `fetch`, and configuration."],[1005,"`@osdk/client`, `@osdk/api`, and `@osdk/react` are released together and share a version number. `@osdk/react` imports its cache runtime from `@osdk/client/observable`, which is tied to the exact release line, so a mismatch fails at build time even when npm's peer ranges are satisfied."],[558,"`@osdk/foundry-sdk-generator` loads the resources you name from Foundry and writes a compiled npm package with ESM, CommonJS, and bundled type definitions."],[2621,"`@osdk/foundry.admin` reads and manages Foundry users, groups, and markings. Use it to show who is signed in, resolve user IDs to names, check group membership, and list the markings a user can view."],[1954,"`@osdk/foundry.aipagents` is the Platform SDK package for AIP Agents. It works with the `Client` from `createClient` or a `PlatformClient`; see Platform SDK setup."],[2350,"`@osdk/foundry.datasets` reads and writes Foundry datasets. Use it to download a file, export a table as CSV or Arrow, upload files, and group several uploads into one transaction."],[2474,"`@osdk/foundry.mediasets` works with media sets: Foundry collections of images, documents, audio, video, and other files. Use it to upload items to a media set, download them, read their metadata, and convert them with transformations."],[2540,"`@osdk/foundry.ontologies` also contains the raw object, link, action, and query endpoints that the OSDK calls. Prefer the OSDK for those: it adds types, pagination helpers, caching in React, and result conversion."],[2532,"`@osdk/foundry.ontologies` reads the schema of any Ontology at runtime: its object types, properties, link types, action types, and query types. Use it for tools that work across object types you do not know at build time, such as schema browsers, admin pages, and code generators."],[2449,"`@osdk/foundry` re-exports each package except `@osdk/foundry.geojson` as a namespace, such as `Admin` and `Datasets`. Prefer the individual packages: they version independently and keep your bundle small."],[646,"`@osdk/integration-testing` is a `0.x` package and depends on the experimental `--experimentalOntologyMetadata` generator flag. Its API may change between releases."],[645,"`@osdk/integration-testing` starts a local Ontology server from the Foundry CLI, fills it with seed objects and links, and gives you a real `Client` connected to it. Use it to test code that queries objects, filters, and follows links end to end, without a Foundry stack. To test React components with mocks instead, see Testing."],[2121,"`@osdk/language-models` is not superseded: `@osdk/aip-core` depends on it for the proxy URL. Use `@osdk/aip-core` for a provider-neutral API, and `@osdk/language-models` when you want a provider's own SDK."],[2088,"`@osdk/language-models` is the lower-level package `@osdk/aip-core` is built on. It returns Foundry's provider-compatible proxy URLs, so you can point an OpenAI, Anthropic, or Google SDK at Foundry instead of the provider. Use it when you need a provider feature `@osdk/aip-core` does not cover yet, such as the OpenAI Responses API or embeddings."],[1032,"`@osdk/maker-import` turns the metadata of an ontology that already exists in Foundry into TypeScript modules. Import those modules into your `.ontology/ontology.mts` file to link to, implement, or reuse existing types without redefining them."],[1080,"`@osdk/maker` installs a `maker` binary. For local development, also install the Vite plugin; see Local development."],[3711,"`@osdk/oauth`"],[727,"`@osdk/react-components` changelog"],[6072,"`@osdk/react-components` follows the same pattern as `@osdk/react-components`:"],[5946,"`@osdk/react-components` ships built-in dark-theme overrides that activate when `[data-bp-color-scheme=\"dark\"]` is set on an ancestor element (typically `<html>`) — matching Blueprint's own convention so the OSDK + Blueprint tokens flip together. The supported way to set the attribute is `<OsdkThemeProvider>` from `@osdk/react-components/experimental/theme`; see OsdkThemeProvider for the full reference, including `defaultTheme=\"system\"` (the default, follows `prefers-color-scheme`), runtime toggles via `useOsdkTheme`, and controlled mode for integrating with an external theme store."],[712,"`@osdk/react-components`, `@osdk/react-devtools`, and `@osdk/oauth` have their own version numbers. Upgrade them alongside the three core packages. See Version compatibility."],[2899,"`@osdk/react-devtools` adds a panel to your `@osdk/react` app during development. Use it to see which components own OSDK hooks, how often reads hit the cache, and what your app logs to the console."],[2900,"`@osdk/react-devtools` is in beta. Its panels and metrics can change between minor versions."],[977,"`@osdk/react-devtools` ships a Vite plugin that injects an inspection panel into your app during `vite dev` and does nothing in production builds:"],[3709,"`@osdk/react/platform-apis`"],[3708,"`@osdk/react`"],[2178,"`@osdk/react` already depends on `@osdk/aip-core`; install it directly because your code imports `foundryModel` from it. `useChat` does not read from `OsdkProvider`, so it works inside or outside one."],[725,"`@osdk/react` changelog"],[792,"`@osdk/react` does not touch the DOM, so its hooks work in React Native screens. The template lists `@osdk/react` in `package.json` but does not use it, and pins it to `^0.7.0`, a release line from before `@osdk/react` shared a version number with `@osdk/client`. Install the same version as your `@osdk/client` first, for example:"],[217,"`@osdk/react` hooks can return data and report loading at the same time. This page shows the loading pattern that avoids flashing UI, the error shape of each hook, and how to turn an error into a message the user can act on."],[4467,"`@osdk/react` is built on this client. Its hooks accept the same `where` clauses, derived property definitions, and action parameters, and add a shared cache that keeps components current after actions and live updates. In a React app, use the hooks for data you render and call the client directly for one-off work, such as exports. See Objects in React."],[820,"`@osdk/react` is not needed in Node."],[149,"`@osdk/react` keeps one normalized cache per `OsdkProvider`. This page explains what the cache stores, when hooks hit the network, and how data updates after actions, so you can predict what a component renders."],[1003,"`@osdk/react` supports React 17, 18, and 19. For prebuilt components, also install `@osdk/react-components`; its setup is on Components prerequisites."],[3008,"`@osdk/react` wraps the OSDK client in hooks. Every hook reads from one normalized cache, so a list, a detail view, and a link panel that show the same object stay in sync, and an action updates them all without manual refetching."],[4544,"`@osdk/react` wraps these calls in hooks that cache and update: `useLinks` for `$link`, and `useObjectSet` for pivots and set operations. See Links in React and Object sets in React."],[1348,"`@osdk/seed-helpers` and `@osdk/seed-compiler` build seed data for a different local server: the Ontology server that `@osdk/integration-testing` starts, which loads seed JSON into SQLite. They do not seed the Vite plugin's mock. To use them in tests, see Integration testing."],[3377,"`@osdk/unit-testing` reference"],[377,"`@osdk/vite-plugin-branch`, registered in `vite.config.ts` for the React and Vue 2.x templates. The Expo template instead passes `UNSTABLE_DO_NOT_USE_BRANCH: $branch` to `createClient`."],[1313,"`@osdk/vite-plugin-oac` lets you build an app against your ontology-as-code definitions before they exist in Foundry. On every change to `.ontology/`, it compiles the ontology again, regenerates an SDK into `.osdk/src`, and answers OSDK requests from the Vite dev server with data from a seed file."],[1897,"`@osdk/widget.client-react` connects a React tree to the Foundry host. `FoundryWidget` handles the message protocol, and `useFoundryWidgetContext` gives every component typed parameter values and an `emitEvent` function derived from your widget config."],[1899,"`@osdk/widget.client-react` supports React 18 and 19 and lists `@osdk/client` as a peer dependency. Add `@osdk/client`, `@osdk/react`, and your SDK package to call the Ontology."],[1822,"`@osdk/widget.vite-plugin` writes the manifest during `vite build` and runs developer mode during `vite`. Projects from `@osdk/create-widget` already include it."],[1245,"`<object>` is the object type's API name in lowercase with hyphens, so `Employee` gives `modify-object-employee`. Parameters skip vector properties, properties in `excludedProperties`, and properties set by `nonParameterMappings`; all three also skip properties backed by a `derived` datasource. For interfaces, see Interface actions."],[6476,"`<td>` — Body cell"],[6474,"`<th>` — Header cell"],[6473,"`<thead>` — Table header row container"],[6475,"`<tr>` — Body row"],[4402,"`$and`, `$or`, and `$not` take clauses and nest to any depth:"],[4500,"`$as(Employee)` returns the concrete view of an interface object, with the object type's property names. `$as(Worker)` goes the other way:"],[4501,"`$as` makes no request. It only exposes the properties that were loaded:"],[4505,"`$as` throws when:"],[4653,"`$clone` builds a new local object. It does not write to Foundry; use an action for that. It throws if the changes include a different primary key:"],[315,"`$clone` throws if the update changes the primary key. It does not write anything to Foundry. Use it to build optimistic state for an action, as shown in Optimistic updates. To change data, apply an action."],[4383,"`$containsAnyTerm` and `$containsAllTerms` also take `{ term, fuzzySearch: true }`, which matches terms within a small edit distance, such as a typo:"],[2752,"`$count` is read as `data.$count`. A `\"property:metric\"` key is read as `data.property.metric`, so `\"salary:avg\"` becomes `data.salary.avg`. The result is fully typed from the keys you pass."],[4240,"`$duration` units:"],[4233,"`$groupBy` maps each property to a grouping. Several properties group by every combination of their values."],[4381,"`$in: []` matches every object, not none. When the array comes from user input, check for an empty array before you add the filter."],[3192,"`$in` with an empty array matches every object. If the list comes from user input, set `enabled: false` (see Fetch conditionally) or leave the filter out when the list is empty."],[4504,"`$includeAllBaseObjectProperties` cannot be combined with `$select`."],[4385,"`$interval` takes one rule. Rules nest through `$and` and `$or`:"],[4529,"`$link` sends no request until