Action`, tracks pending and validation state, and refreshes cached objects after the action. See Actions in React."],[261,"`useOsdkAction` splits errors into two kinds:"],[2749,"`useOsdkAggregation(type, options)`"],[2750,"`useOsdkAggregation` asks the server for counts, sums, averages, and other metrics over a set of objects, optionally grouped by property values. Only the results come back, so a total over a million objects costs one small response."],[4250,"`useOsdkAggregation` from `@osdk/react` runs the same request, caches the result, and loads it again when objects of the type change. See Aggregations in React."],[3835,"`useOsdkClient()` returns the underlying client used by the observable client's store."],[4448,"`useOsdkFunction` from `@osdk/react` executes a function, caches the result by its parameters, and can refetch when objects it depends on change. See Functions in React."],[2999,"`useOsdkFunctions` runs a list of function calls and returns one result per call, in the same order. Each result has the same shape as the `useOsdkFunction` return value."],[259,"`useOsdkObject(Employee, id)` for an object that does not exist, or that you cannot see, reports a `PalantirApiError` with the message `Object not found: <id>` and no `errorCode` or `statusCode`."],[161,"`useOsdkObject(Employee, id)` with a type and primary key does fetch. Calls for the same object type made within 25 ms are batched into one request of up to 100 objects."],[160,"`useOsdkObject(instance)` reads the object from the cache and does not send a request. Use it in a row component to get `isOptimistic` for an object a list already loaded:"],[3175,"`useOsdkObject`"],[3228,"`useOsdkObject` also returns a `forceUpdate` function, but it is not implemented and throws when called. Cache invalidation covers the other invalidation methods."],[3222,"`useOsdkObject` takes an object type and a primary key, and returns `object`:"],[3174,"`useOsdkObjects(type, options?)`"],[4683,"`useOsdkObjects` and `useObjectSet` accept `streamUpdates: true`, except together with `pivotTo` or `withProperties`, which subscribes for you and writes each change into the shared cache. See Objects in React."],[3111,"`useOsdkObjects` and `useOsdkAggregation` take an `intersectWith` option instead: an array of `{ where }` clauses on the same type. Use it when every set you would intersect is a filter on that type, and `useObjectSet` when the sets come from elsewhere, such as a pivot."],[3176,"`useOsdkObjects` loads a list of objects of one type, and `useOsdkObject` loads a single object. Both read from the shared cache that `OsdkProvider` sets up, so every component that shows the same object re-renders when it changes."],[6785,"`useOsdkTheme` hook"],[2634,"`Users.get` takes a user ID. Pass `status: \"DELETED\"` to resolve a user who has been removed, for example the author of an old record:"],[2623,"`Users.getCurrent` returns the user the token belongs to:"],[2637,"`Users.list` lists every user, one page at a time. Its query parameters:"],[2642,"`Users.profilePicture` returns the image as a `Response`:"],[2636,"`Users.search` runs a case-insensitive prefix search on username, given name, and family name. It only returns active users:"],[3097,"`useStableObjectSet(objectSet)`"],[3340,"`useUserViewMarkings` returns the IDs of the markings a user can view. Without `userId`, it uses the signed-in user:"],[2721,"`validateAction` asks the server whether a set of parameters would pass, without applying the action. An `INVALID` result is not an error: it resolves normally and sets `validationResult`. `validateAction` rejects only when the request itself fails, so it needs a `catch` like `applyAction`."],[2725,"`validateAction` does nothing and resolves to `undefined` while `applyAction` is running."],[269,"`validateAction` runs the same validation without applying the action. An invalid result does not throw: it resolves to the validation response, which the hook also stores as `validationResult`. `validateAction` throws only for other failures, and resolves to `undefined` if a newer validation or an `applyAction` call supersedes it."],[2732,"`validateAction` takes one parameter object, not an array."],[2727,"`validationResult` keeps the last completed result, so it can describe older input. It is not updated during the debounce delay, and `isValidating` can turn `false` while a newer validation is still running. Treat it as a hint for the UI: `applyAction` validates again on the server and reports a failure in `error.actionValidation`."],[1797,"`vite build` writes one manifest per widget set to `.palantir/widgets.config.json` in the build output. Each widget's config becomes an entry under `widgetSet.widgets`, keyed by widget ID. For the config at the top of this page, the manifest looks like this:"],[970,"`vite dev` runs in `development` mode and `vite build` in `production` mode, so production bundles get `\"production\"`."],[943,"`VITE_FOUNDRY_REDIRECT_URL` must exactly match a redirect URL on **OAuth & scopes** in Developer Console. Authentication covers scopes, token storage, and sign-out, all of which work the same in Vue."],[939,"`vite.config.ts` aliases `vue` to `vue/dist/vue.esm-bundler`, the build that includes the template compiler, because `src/main.ts` mounts the root component from a template string (`\"<router-view />\"`). Keep the alias, or change `main.ts` to mount a compiled `.vue` component instead."],[955,"`watchEffect` resubscribes when `department()` changes and unsubscribes when the component unmounts. Call `useEmployees(() => props.department)` from `<script setup>`."],[3130,"`where` can reference derived properties defined in the same options. For TypeScript to infer them, annotate the builder parameter with `DerivedProperty.Builder` from `@osdk/client`:"],[4355,"`where` narrows an object set on the server. It takes a clause object and returns a new object set, so you can chain it with other `where` calls, links, aggregations, and any load method."],[4356,"`where` sends no request by itself. The filter runs when you call `fetchPage`, `asyncIter`, `aggregate`, or another method that loads data."],[3180,"`where` takes the same clause as `ObjectSet.where` in `@osdk/client`. A bare value on a property means equality:"],[2759,"`where` takes the same clause as `useOsdkObjects`. `intersectWith` adds clauses that every object must also match."],[4258,"`withProperties` adds properties that the server computes at query time, such as an employee's number of reports or their lead's name. You can select, filter, sort, and aggregate on them like stored properties, and nothing is written to the Ontology."],[2760,"`withProperties` defines derived properties that `where` can filter on."],[3115,"`withProperties` defines properties that the server computes for each object and returns alongside its regular properties. Pass it to `useOsdkObjects` or `useObjectSet`. Each entry is a function that receives a builder for the object and returns a definition:"],[4259,"`withProperties` takes an object that maps each new property name to a function. The function receives a builder for the object it computes on, and returns a definition. The result is a new object set with the new properties typed: `reportCount` is a `number` and `leadName` is `string | undefined`."],[1144,"`Worker` inherits `name` from `Party`, so an object type that implements `Worker` must provide `name` and `jobTitle`, and may provide `email` and `hourlyRate`."],[7121,"`WorkshopModule` inherits the initial theme from the nearest OsdkThemeProvider. The provider is optional; without one, Workshop uses the module's theme. In `system` mode, the iframe stays hidden with no URL until the browser preference is resolved. It starts loading only after the initial light or dark theme is known."],[7118,"`WorkshopModule` inherits the resolved global branch from the client in `OsdkProvider`, so the embedded module uses the same branch as the app's OSDK requests.