erated from your object types, so `Employee` has `fullName` and `startDate`. | Generic request and response types from the public API, such as `User` and `Dataset`. |"],[3633,"| `$applyModifiers?` | `({ [P in Exclude<PropsWithMainValue<Q>, PropsWithReducers<Q>>]?: \"applyMainValue\" \\| undefined; } & { [P in Exclude<PropsWithReducers<Q>, PropsWithMainValue<...>>]?: \"applyReducers\" \\| undefined; } & ... & MODIFIERS & ...)` | Applies ontology-configured reducers or struct main values to properties by API name. Only properties with the corresponding configuration in the generated SDK accept a modifier: - `\"applyReducers\"`: reduces an array to a single element using its configured reducers. - `\"applyMainValue\"`: extracts a struct's configured main value. One main-value field returns that field's value; multiple fields return a struct containing only those fields. - `\"applyReducersAndExtractMainValue\"`: reduces a struct array, then extracts its main value. Requires both reducers and a main value. When passing `$select`, include every modified property using its plain API name, along with any other properties you need. Unlisted properties are not modified by this option. The returned property types reflect the modifiers, preserving property nullability. For an explicit `Osdk.Instance` type, use `\"propertyApiName:modifier\"` in its property keys. |\n| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------