es natively: 22.18 or later, or 23.6 or later. On older versions, write the file as `.mjs`."],[441,"The CLI looks for `foundry.config.json` in the current directory and then each parent, and uses the first one it finds. A file holds either a `site` block or a `widgetSet` block, not both. The full schema is in Host on Foundry."],[552,"The CLI opens your browser to sign in, with `http://localhost:8080/auth/callback` as the redirect URL, so allow that URL on **OAuth & scopes** and keep port 8080 free. It generates every object type, action, function, and interface the client can see in the Ontology, except functions that return Ontology edits. If the client can see more than one Ontology, pass `--ontologyRid`. Pass `--branch <branch RID>` to read a branch's Ontology."],[617,"The CLI prints a preview link, `https://<subdomain>/.system/preview?previewVersion=<version>`. When it looks right, make it live:"],[594,"The CLI reads it from the current directory or the nearest parent, so you do not repeat these values on every command. All fields are described in `foundry.config.json`."],[1871,"The CLI reads the version from `.palantir/widgets.config.json` in the directory, zips everything in the directory, and uploads it. When `repository` is set, it uploads to that code repository on behalf of the widget set, which works only if the repository is authorized to publish the widget set. It passes the current git branch along, unless HEAD is detached or the project is not a git repository."],[862,"The CLI still asks for a Foundry URL, application RID, client ID, application URL, CORS proxy, and scopes, and writes `.env.development`, `.env.production`, `.npmrc`, and `foundry.config.json` from them. The library does not read the `.env` files or `foundry.config.json`; delete them if you do not need them. Keep `.npmrc` if you will install your SDK from the Foundry registry. It holds only the token line, so add your SDK's `@scope:registry=` line as described in Installation."],[470,"The CLI turns Foundry errors into these messages:"],[21,"The client calls `auth()` before each request. You can call it yourself: `await auth()` resolves to the current access token and starts sign-in when there is none."],[76,"The client does not refresh a static token. When it expires, requests fail with `401` until you supply a new one."],[4563,"The client does not write files to objects directly. Pass the file as an action parameter, and the client uploads it before it applies the action:"],[88,"The client from `@osdk/client` sends every OSDK request: it fetches objects, applies actions, and executes functions. Create it once per app, then hand it to `OsdkProvider` for React or call it directly."],[41,"The client refreshes the access token one minute before it expires. You do not schedule refreshes yourself. With `tokenStorage: \"none\"` there is no refresh token, so users sign in again when the access token expires."],[71,"The client requests a new token when the current one expires. There is no refresh token or browser storage. It supports `signIn()`, `signOut()`, `getTokenOrUndefined()`, and `signIn` and `signOut` events, but not `refresh()`."],[2331,"The client retries `429` and `503` responses up to three times before rejecting. Other errors reject immediately."],[4324,"The client retries a request that fails with `429 Too Many Requests` or `503 Service Unavailable`, up to three times. The delays grow exponentially from about one second, with random jitter. Other errors, including network failures, are not retried. Your code sees the error only after the last attempt."],[260,"The client retries requests that fail with `429` or `503` up to three times, with exponential backoff, before the error reaches your hook."],[46,"The client starts sign-in on the first request, so pages that load OSDK data trigger it automatically. To keep a route from rendering until the user is signed in, check for a token first. `getTokenOrUndefined()` returns the current access toke